A||||9||fiero56||Duke w/p pulley pressed on||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||12-01-2001||02:14 PM||||Trying to replace the water pump on an '87 coupe. I've got the replacement water pump with no pulley, just the shaft, and the old w/p with pulley still attached. This pulley appears to be pressed on to the shaft, and I haven't had any luck finding anyone who can swap the pulleys. <P>How is this done? How do others swap pulleys?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||12-01-2001||03:20 PM||||It's pressed, the puller grabs the rim on the pulley hub. It's made specifically for this job. press is mainly a nut bolt and washers.<P>To make it more complicated.... the pulley usually needs to be adjusted once the pump is in the car. Especially on cars with AC. The serpent belt won't last long unless the pulleys are carfully aligned. It's not quite as critical on non AC cars but still important.<P>That pump doesn't come with the pulley because there are several diferant ones that can be used depending on which car/options the motor is in.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-01-2001||04:06 PM||||I had the guys at AutoZone try every puller they had, nothing really grabbed like it should. Is there a specific puller for this purpose?<P>This car has a/c too. Haynes manual doesn't give any info. How should I align this once installed?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||84sleepr||12-01-2001||09:12 PM||||I replaced my waterpump on my other fiero about 2 months ago.  They gave me a w/p with a 3 groove pulley already pressed on.  I only had the 1 groove pulley but they told me the replacement w/p only came with the 3 groove.  It was kind of a *****  to replace cus the pulley overlapped some of the w/p bolts but it didn't require any adjustment to align the belt.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ITB 2M4||12-01-2001||11:54 PM||||Luckily, a friend of mine used to be a mechanic, now he's a service writer.  He had a special tool just for water pumps.  It also sets the pulley on to just the right height.  I think it was a MAC or Snap-On special tool.  When I used to work for a Chevy dealer, I know we had special tools that we could order for customers as well.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||10
Z||000005||theogre||12-02-2001||12:09 AM||||Yes, there is a special tool for this work. It's made to fit these pulleys. No, I'm not sure where to get it. <A HREF="http://www.lislecorp.com" TARGET=_blank>www.lislecorp.com</A>  a puller you can get thru <A HREF="http://www.ontool.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ontool.com</A> <P>It the 38850 here.... <A HREF="http://www.lislecorp.com/tools/catalog/pullers/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lislecorp.com/tools/catalog/pullers/index.htm</A>  and here... <A HREF="http://www.ontool.com/store/category/1270?OntoolSession=PAms91Dg52KjjvOQvXFevZKZcZ1YiQPAXHhXk2Cx4vs0cTJHpcui|-8646115538672851614/-1062723083/5/7001/7001/7002/7002/-1" TARGET=_blank>http://w  ww.ontool.com/store/category/1270?OntoolSession=PAms91Dg52KjjvOQvXFevZKZcZ1YiQPAXHhXk2Cx4vs0cTJHpcui|||209.246.210.19||reg||1
Z||000006||SLYGUY||12-02-2001||01:05 AM||||When I replaced the alternator in my 88GT I bought a higher amp rebuilt at NAPA. Turned out the rebuilt one was assembled with all of the mounting bolts/plug on the opposite side that I needed. <BR>They "re-clocked" this alternator so that it would line up as well as removing my pulley from the old unit and installing it on the new one. <BR>See if they can help.<P>Just my $.02<P>Sly<p>[This message has been edited by SLYGUY (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||12-02-2001||01:17 AM||||I'm not sure where in southwestern Ohio you are, but if you can't find anyplace to do it, I can. All that would need to happen is to get the parts to me. <BR>PITA for you though.<P>I would think that any machine shop in the area should be able to help you out though.<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||jelly2m8||12-02-2001||02:41 AM|||| Some water pumps come with a pulley some don't. Guess it all depends if the core was sent back with one or not, and the mood of the guy remanufacturing it.<P> Align the pulley after its on the car?.<P> If the pulley was installed correctly using the pulley remove / installation tool, there should be no need of having to adjust it.<P> If you don't have access to this tool, take the pumps to a garage, or the GM dealer, they should only charge you a couple of dollars to swap it.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||12-02-2001||09:39 AM||||Still no luck. I will go to PepBoys today and see what they can do. I've got the old pump out, just need the pulley transferred.||||reg||1
